  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1119 PM CDT Sun Jul 26 2026\n\nTonight and Monday...\n\nAnother very warm and humid day unfolded beneath the persistent mid- \nto upper-level ridge. High temperatures reached the lower 100s at \nmany locations, while continued low-level moisture transport and \nevapotranspiration maintained dewpoints in the upper 70s to low 80s. \nThis resulted in peak heat index values of 115 to 120 degrees. \n\nUnder performing convection this evening should allow for minimal \ncloud cover into Monday morning, allowing another scorching day \nacross the area. However, a shortwave disturbance rounding the ridge \nwill drive a surface front southward through the region. Winds \nbehind the front will shift from southerly to northeasterly, \nreducing low-level moisture transport and gradually lowering \ndewpoints, but bringing in wildfire smoke with some near-surface \nsmoke expected to filter in through the day. Little temperature drop \nis expected behind the front, in fact, the drier air mass may warm \nefficiently during the day as a greater share of the available energy \ngoes toward sensible heating. High temperatures will again reach the \nupper 90s to lower 100s areawide. Dewpoints will vary\nconsiderably across the area, remaining in the upper 70s/low \n80s across southwest Iowa while falling into the mid 60s across\nnortheast Nebraska behind the front. As as result, peak heat \nindex values are expected to reach 110 to 115 degrees across \nsouthwest Iowa, with values generally near 105 degrees \nelsewhere. An Extreme Heat Warning remains in effect across \nsoutheast Nebraska and southwest Iowa, with a Heat Advisory in \neffect farther north.\n\nMonday evening into early Tuesday, another shortwave disturbance \nmoving from the Front Range into western Nebraska will initiate a \nround of thunderstorms to our west, with activity progressing \neastward. Instability will remain plentiful, though shear is \nexpected to weaken as the storms approach the forecast area. Even so, \nforecast soundings show a favorable wind profile and DCAPE values \nnear 1250 J/kg, supporting the low-end potential for isolated wind \ngusts of 45 to 60 mph if storms are able to maintain themselves into \neastern Nebraska. PoPs currently peak at 15-30%.\n\nTuesday and Beyond...\n\nThe aforementioned shortwave disturbance will dampen the ridge and \nbring temperatures down on Tuesday, though highs will still reach \nthe low 90s and remain slightly above seasonal norms. Dewpoints will \nalso decrease into the upper 60s/low 70s. A few lingering showers \nmay continued Tuesday morning before gradually diminishing.\n\nFor the remainder of the work week, highs will generally range from \nthe upper 80s to lower 90s. Additional shortwave disturbances \nrounding the ridge will bring periodic precipitation chances. \nConfidence continues to increase in a more widespread round of \nshowers and storms Thursday into Friday as a stronger shortwave\ndisturbances crosses the northern Plains. PoPs of 60-75% are \ncurrently in place, with the cloud cover keeping highs to the \nmid 80s. Potential exists for a few strong to severe storms, \nwith GEFS and EPS based machine learning guidance painting a \n5-15% probability from Nebraska into the Dakotas. We'll have to \nmonitor how the details shake out as it approaches.",
